# Source novels — NEVER commit.
#
# These are copyrighted texts supplied by the user for local coursework. Git
# retains blobs in history even after a later deletion, so an accidental commit
# is not cleanly reversible. Everything downstream is designed around this:
# data/gold/ stores character offsets and short evidence snippets rather than
# chapter text, precisely so annotations stay shareable without the novels.
data/raw/
*.epub
*.pdf
*.mobi
*.azw3

# Built graph databases — regenerate with `echotales run`.
data/*.db
data/*.db-wal
data/*.db-shm
data/echotales.db*

# Working copies the webview backend edits, so a correction can never mutate
# the originals above. Regenerate with the copy step in HANDOFF §4.18.
data/webview-working/

# Generated review artifacts (contain short text snippets from the sources).
data/review/
data/gold/*.jsonl
data/webview/

# React viewer (packages/pipeline/.../webview.py generates its data)
webview/node_modules/
webview/build/
webview/public/data/

# Induced lexicons are regenerable; keep the genre-neutral seed tracked.
data/lexicons/*.toml
!data/lexicons/_seed.toml

# Chapter-NER cache (mentions/chapter_ner.py::NameCache) -- pure cache, keyed
# by a hash of chapter text, regenerates on the next LLM-backed run.
data/lexicons/*-ner-cache.json

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
htmlcov/
.coverage
.coverage.*
dist/
build/

# Environment
.env
.env.local
# Machine-specific (e.g. a local gateway port) -- config.json.example is
# the committed template, same pattern as .env/.env.example.
config.json

# Editors / OS
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# Local-only Claude Code working rules — never share/commit.
.claude

# Phase 8: voice bank (~11 GB corpus) and synthesised audio. Both are
# regenerable -- the bank by re-downloading VCTK, the audio by re-running
# `echotales voice` -- and neither belongs in git.
#
# Wildcarded (`data/audio*/` not `data/audio/`), because every scratch/testing
# rerun in this project has landed in a suffixed sibling directory --
# `data/audio_real/`, `data/panels_v2/`, `data/panels_v3/`, `data/video_v3/`
# -- so an exact-name-only rule silently stopped covering the very output it
# was written for the moment anyone iterated on it. If a real, permanent
# output directory needs tracking, `!data/whatever/` after this block is the
# right way to carve out that exception explicitly, not renaming the rule
# back to an exact match.
data/voice*/
data/audio*/
data/reruns*/
# Phase 9 render output. Regenerable with `echotales render`, and binary --
# panels are one PNG per block (thousands per novel), video is one mp4 per
# chapter. Same reasoning as data/audio*/ above, same wildcarding.
data/panels*/
data/motion*/
data/references*/
data/video*/

# Ad hoc experiment / scratch databases that aren't the canonical per-novel
# run (that's `data/<novel>.db`, matched by the `data/*.db` rule above).
# Session scratch work has repeatedly landed here as `data/*-body.db`,
# `data/*-split.db` and similar throwaway copies used to test a code change
# against real data without touching the canonical graph.
data/*-body.db
data/*-body.db-*
data/*-split.db
data/*-split.db-*

# Per-novel output roots (pipeline/paths.py::novel_root -> data/RI, data/LOTM,
# data/ORV, ...). The rules above matched the old flat layout -- data/panels*,
# data/video*, data/audio* -- and stopped covering anything the moment output
# moved under a novel directory. Matched one asset kind at a time rather than
# the whole novel dir, so a novel's README/manifest stays trackable while its
# 1.9 GB of PNGs and mp4s does not.
data/*/panels/
data/*/video/
data/*/audio/
data/*/motion/
data/*/references/
data/*/scratch/
data/diag/

# Hand-collected composition references (Pinterest fan art). Deliberately not
# committed: these are other artists' copyrighted work, gathered as local
# conditioning input. Pushing them to a public repo is redistribution, which
# is a different act from using them locally -- same reasoning as data/raw/.
data/scene-references/
data/voice/cremad/
